<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    THE GATE ZERO SCHEDULER CANARY, AND IT IS WRITTEN DOWN UNINSTALLED.

    This agent is listed in DEFINITION_ONLY in ops/config-as-code.py, so its
    PRESENCE in ~/Library/LaunchAgents is a drift finding until Joe removes it
    from that list deliberately. Activating a schedule is a human act; this
    file is the definition of what would be activated, in the repo, where the
    rest of the machine's config already lives.

    WHAT IT IS FOR. Gate Zero's fourth predecessor step,
    `step:scheduler-active-receipt`, asks whether the scheduler is really
    running. The reader that judges it lives in
    mcp-server/src/gate-zero-seam-readers.v5.js and wants an ops.run row whose
    evidence_ref is not null, written by bin/run-scheduled.sh, plus a later
    observation of that same run key and receipt. Nothing in the ledger could
    supply that: on 2026-09-11 production held 28,309 runs, 21,894 of them from
    this wrapper, and not one carrying an evidence_ref.

    WHY A DISTINCT RUN KEY. Every other wrapper-driven agent passes
    `launchd.run`, so the ledger cannot tell one job's dispatch from another's
    by key alone. This one passes `gatezero.canary`, which is the key the gate
    is pointed at and which nothing else writes.

    WHY A RECEIPT FILE AND NOT A CONSTANT. The receipt path is handed to the
    job AND to the wrapper (the wrapper's receipt flag is spelled out in
    ProgramArguments below rather than here: a double hyphen is illegal inside
    an XML comment, and writing one is what made notes-sweep the single plist
    in this directory no strict XML parser could read, found 2026-08-14 by
    tools/scheduler-truth.py). The job mints a fresh token per run and writes
    it there; the wrapper reads it back after the child exits and binds the row
    to it. A constant receipt would let a row from last month answer a question
    about today, which is the exact failure this canary exists to rule out.

    THE CADENCE IS HOURLY because the gate wants a recent dispatch, not a
    frequent one, and because every fire costs one spooled row. The service's
    declared grace in ops/config/services.json is sized for the 30-minute
    run-spool flush on top of that interval.
  -->
  <key>Label</key><string>com.carr.gate-zero-canary</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/zsh</string>
    <string>{{REPO}}/bin/run-scheduled.sh</string>
    <string>--evidence-ref-file</string>
    <string>{{REPO}}/out/gate-zero-canary.receipt</string>
    <string>gate-zero-canary</string>
    <string>gatezero.canary</string>
    <string>/bin/zsh</string>
    <string>{{REPO}}/bin/gate-zero-canary.sh</string>
    <string>{{REPO}}/out/gate-zero-canary.receipt</string>
  </array>

  <key>StartInterval</key><integer>3600</integer>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><true/>
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key><integer>60</integer>

  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PATH</key><string>/opt/homebrew/opt/node@22/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin</string>
  </dict>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>{{REPO}}/out/gate-zero-canary-launchd.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>{{REPO}}/out/gate-zero-canary-launchd.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
